  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nRest of Today-Wednesday...High pressure over the eastern US\nweakens and moves offshore through Wednesday ahead of a low\npressure system dropping through the Great Lakes. Light winds\nprevail this afternoon, with a low (20%) chance for a few showers\nsouth of Orlando over the interior into this evening. A mid-level\nshortwave is forecast to traverse the Florida peninsula on \nWednesday, supporting a slight increase in PoPs. PWATs >1.5\" from \naround Melbourne southward and energy aloft are expected to \ngenerate scattered showers and storms Wednesday afternoon, while \nareas to the north remain dry. A few strong storms will be \npossible, with modest lapse rates and 500 mb temperatures near -10\nto -11 C. The main threats will be gusty winds 45-50 mph and \nhail. Temperatures remain above normal in the lower to mid-80s \nduring the daytime hours. However, will see lows tonight in the \nupper 60s to lower 70s fall to the upper 50s to mid-60s Wednesday \nnight.\n\nThursday-Tuesday...The low pressure system moves through the \nNortheast US late week and deepens. Locally, a pre-frontal trough \nlooks to pass through the Florida peninsula on Thursday, providing\na precursor reduction in available moisture, humidity, and \ntemperatures. PoPs decrease area-wide for Thursday, with just a \nfew lingering showers possible along the southern Treasure Coast \n(20%) due to continued moisture across south Florida. High \ntemperatures cool to nearer to normal, in the upper 70s to near \n80, with overnight lows dipping into the mid to upper 50s from \nnear Orlando northward. Westerly winds Thursday increase to around\n10-15 mph.\n\nHowever, the biggest change will follow the main, stronger cold \nfront on Friday. Much drier air behind the front will decrease\nPWATs to 0.5\" or less. Thus, PoPs below 10% continue through the\nweekend. Northwesterly winds on Friday veer northerly on \nSaturday, ushering in cooler temperatures. Highs in the upper 60s \nnorth of I-4 and in the lower 70s elsewhere on Friday fall \nfurther, into the mid to upper 60s on Saturday. Overnight lows\nFriday night and Saturday night fall into the mid to upper 40s\nacross the normally cooler locations, while the coast remains in\nthe lower 50s. But, winds hovering around 5-10 mph overnight will\nproduce wind chills in the lower to mid-40s inland. Moisture and\ntemperatures rebound Sunday and into early next week, as ridging\nbuilds into the eastern US. Temperatures slowly climb to near\nnormal through the period, with low to medium (20-40%) PoPs\nreturning.",
